OPENSSL  

This is the standard command used to initiate a secure TLS handshake with a web server:bash
openssl s_client -connect <domain_or_IP>:<port> -servername <domain>
Detailed Syntax Breakdown:
openssl: Calls the OpenSSL command-line program.
s_client: The specific sub-command/utility that acts as a generic SSL/TLS client.
-connect: Specifies the target address and port.
<domain_or_IP>:<port>: The destination. For secure web browsing (HTTPS), the port is always 443.if you want any other port to send data or something , should specify the port after the domain with a semicolon . 
-servername: Sends the hostname during the TLS handshake (known as SNI). 
This is critical because modern servers host multiple websites on a single IP address.<domain>: 
The exact website name (e.g., google.com).


Enforce a Specific TLS Protocol Version
You can force the command to connect using only a specific version of TLS by appending a protocol flag:bash
openssl s_client -connect google.com:443 -servername google.com -tls1_2
openssl s_client -connect google.com:443 -servername google.com -tls1_3

If, when connecting to a remote server in this way, the TLS handshake completes but you’re getting disconnected after the first HTTP request line, check that you’ve specified the -crlf switch on the command line. This switch ensures that the newlines you type are translated to a carriage return plus line feed combo to ensure string HTTP compliance.


Using:

openssl s_client -connect bandit.labs.overthewire.org:31790 -quiet

suppresses those TLS messages, which is why the Bandit hint specifically points you toward the "CONNECTED COMMANDS" section of the man page.


Nmap


Nmap follows the general syntax nmap [Scan Type] [Options] {target}, where you specify the type of scan, any additional options, and the target host or network. Ports can be selected using the -p flag. To scan all 65,535 ports, use nmap -p- 192.168.1.1; to scan a range of ports, use nmap -p 1-100 192.168.1.1; and to scan specific ports, use nmap -p 22,80,443 192.168.1.1. Nmap can also scan both TCP and UDP ports simultaneously, for example sudo nmap -sS -sU -p T:21,22,80,U:53,161 192.168.1.1, where -sS performs a TCP SYN scan and -sU performs a UDP scan. For a quicker scan of only the 100 most common ports, use nmap -F 192.168.1.1. Targets may be specified as a single IP address (nmap -p- 192.168.1.50), a domain name (nmap -p- scanme.nmap.org), an IP range (nmap -p 1-100 192.168.1.1-50), or an entire subnet using CIDR notation (nmap -p 80,443 192.168.1.0/24). Common scan options include -sS for a fast TCP SYN scan, -sV for service and version detection, -O for operating system detection, -Pn to skip host discovery and treat the target as online, and -v for verbose output. A typical enumeration workflow involves first discovering open ports with nmap -p- <target> and then performing detailed service enumeration using nmap -sC -sV -p <open_ports> <target>, where -sC runs Nmap's default scripting engine checks and -sV identifies service versions running on the discovered ports.


SSH

The Standard CommandOpen your terminal and run the following command, replacing the placeholder information with your actual details:bash
ssh -i /path/to/private_key username@remote_host
/path/to/private_key: The exact filepath to your private key file (e.g., ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 or ~/Downloads/my-key.pem).
username: Your user account name on the remote server (e.g., ubuntu or root).
remote_host: The IP address or domain name of the destination server.


EDIT 

The Linux diff command compares two files or directories line by line to show the exact differences between them. 
It is widely used by developers and system administrators to track changes, review code modifications, and generate patch files.
Basic Syntax
diff [options] file1 file2
Use code with caution.
If both files are identical, diff outputs absolutely nothing.
If they differ, diff generates instructions showing how to change file1 to make it exactly match file2


Netcat
nc -help – This command will print a list of all of the available commands you can use in Netcat. It will come in handy if you run into any errors while writing a script or are unsure of how to proceed.

nc -z -v site.com – This will run a basic port scan of the specified website or server. Netcat will return verbose results with lists of ports and statuses. Keep in mind that you can use an IP address in place of the site domain.

nc -l – This command will instruct the local system to begin listening for TCP connections and UDP activity on a specific port number.


The setuid bit This bit is present for files which have executable permissions. The setuid bit simply indicates that when running the executable, it will set its permissions to that of the user who created it (owner), instead of setting it to the user who launched it. Similarly, there is a
setgid bit which does the same for the gid . To locate the setuid look for an 's' instead of an 'x' in the executable bit of the file permissions. An example of an executable with setuid permission is passwd , as can be seen in the following output.

ls -l /etc/passwd
This returns the following output:

-rwsr-xr-x root root 2447 Aug 29  2018 /etc/passwd

As we can observe, the 'x' is replaced by an 's' in the user section of the file permissions. To set the setuid bit, use the following command.

chmod u+s 

To remove the setuid bit, use the following command.

chmod u-s 

